{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-cb73f5a0-3bf9-49d2-a2c5-5bb7dd2d51b8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cf6474219a84e3f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-x96818bq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go for RHOAI 3.5\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the central unknown of the strategy: **how the default\nPostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio playground RAG is provisioned in RHOAI 3.5**.\nSelf-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern requires a RHOAI-controlled\noperator, which only exists once the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164,\ncurrently In Progress) is delivered — a hard dependency with uncertain timing. The Staff\nEngineer Input directs the development team to define a go/no-go gate for this decision as\npart of sprint planning. The deliverable is a decision record that selects exactly one of\nthree bounded outcomes and gates the corresponding downstream provisioning work.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the go/no-go criteria and decision date for the RHOAI 3.5 provisioning approach,\n  driven by the delivery status and release-fit of the dashboard operator modularization\n  (RHAISTRAT-7164).\n- Assess each bounded outcome against the criteria:\n  - **Outcome A — operator-based self-provisioning**: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer\n    lands in time; PostgreSQL lifecycle management is implemented there following the\n    model-registry-operator pattern.\n  - **Outcome B — BFF-managed provisioning**: the operator layer does not land in time; the\n    gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC and Secret directly\n    (the guardrails-infrastructure pattern), with explicit lifecycle design.\n  - **Outcome C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL**: neither self-provisioning path lands; customers\n    manually configure the OGXServer custom resource with their own PostgreSQL connection\n    details, aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes.\n- Record the release-timing assessment of the hard dependency and the risk accepted by the\n  selected outcome (including the degraded out-of-the-box experience of outcome C).\n- Confirm the scoping model for the selected outcome (per-namespace isolation is the\n  preferred default; the per-instance alternative existed only for Auto Rag, which is out of\n  scope for this strategy).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written decision record exists that selects exactly one outcome (A, B or C) and states\n  the go/no-go criteria, the evidence used, and the decision date.\n- The record documents the delivery-status assessment of the dashboard operator\n  modularization dependency that drove the selection.\n- The record states the fallback that applies if the selected outcome fails later (i.e.,\n  which outcome replaces it), so the switch path is pre-agreed rather than improvised.\n- The selected outcome is confirmed as achievable within the RHOAI 3.5 timeframe by the\n  Gen AI Studio development team (sprint-planning sign-off recorded in the decision record).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — a default vector store available out-of-the-box: this investigation\n  decides *which* provisioning approach delivers that requirement (or explicitly accepts the\n  bring-your-own degradation), gating all provisioning work.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector\n  store: the selected outcome determines the delivery path for this capability.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The go/no-go decision itself is a human sprint-planning/release decision (the Staff\n  Engineer assigns it to the development team); the supporting evidence — dependency status,\n  feasibility and risk of each outcome — is assembled as part of this investigation's work.\n- This epic gates three conditional branch epics (one per outcome) with a\n  `remove` failure impact: under a different outcome, the corresponding downstream epic does\n  not exist.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":53473,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b59d86a4638be6d4","agent_id":"a6f01f67c7f84a90b","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"31f165e1-f81b-4d88-ae63-fc1f2cabe777","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:27:18.539Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
