{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-3332ac34-0da6-44b1-890c-348cb1334ac6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4630b06d12416a7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-x96818bq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E002.md","content":"# Configure the remote pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio OGXServer instances\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the vector-store-side wiring that is common to **every** provisioning\noutcome: configuring the OGX instances created for the Gen AI Studio playground to use the\nremote pgvector vector_io provider (`remote::pgvector`, activated via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`)\ninstead of the removed inline Milvus provider. The existing remote vector store\nimplementation is largely reusable — per the engineering team, the provider integration is\nthe small part of this strategy's effort — so this epic is configuration and wiring work in\nthe gen-ai BFF's OGXServer-instance creation path, not new provider development in the OGX\ndistribution.\n\nThis work is deliberately decoupled from, and runs in parallel with, the provisioning\napproach decision: whichever outcome is selected (operator-based self-provisioning,\nBFF-managed provisioning, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL), the OGX vector I/O configuration is\nthe same — only the source of the PostgreSQL connection string differs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider on OGXServer instances created for the Gen\n  AI Studio playground (activation via the `ENABLE_PGVECTOR` environment guard, following the\n  distribution's conditional provider-activation pattern).\n- Inject the PostgreSQL connection details (host, port, database, credentials) through the\n  existing secret-resolution mechanism (Secret-backed environment variables), so the same\n  wiring works whether the database is provisioned by the operator layer, by the BFF, or\n  supplied by the user.\n- Keep OGX **application storage** untouched: `storage.kv.type` and `storage.sql.type` remain\n  on their existing SQLite default. Vector storage and application storage are independent\n  configurations; there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern in this epic.\n- Preserve the escape hatch: the vector store configuration can be pointed at an external,\n  user-managed vector database instead of the default pgvector instance (users with\n  production-scale vector workloads are directed to an external database).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- An OGXServer instance created for a Gen AI Studio playground activates the remote\n  pgvector vector_io provider, and the provider connects successfully to a PostgreSQL\n  instance with pgvector available when its connection details are supplied.\n- Connection credentials are read from a Kubernetes Secret via the distribution's\n  secret-resolution mechanism — no database credentials appear in plaintext in the\n  OGXServer spec, ConfigMap or logs.\n- The OGX application storage backend remains on its existing default (SQLite) — verified\n  unchanged by this configuration work.\n- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows (document ingestion, embedding, retrieval) complete\n  successfully against a pgvector-backed vector store with no loss of the current inline\n  Milvus capability, when a reachable pgvector endpoint is supplied.\n- Pointing the configuration at an external vector database (connection details swapped)\n  is demonstrated to work — the default is an opinionated default, not a lock-in.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector\n  store with no loss of current capability: this epic is the provider wiring that makes the\n  playground talk to pgvector.\n- **HLR-4 (P0)** — the default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the\n  OGX instance: the remote `remote::pgvector` provider configuration is exactly this\n  decoupling; no inline provider is used.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Strategy open questions on latency thresholds, corpus size and index type are resolved by\n  the Staff Engineer Input as out of scope: the default store targets development\n  experimentation, flat scan (no index) is sufficient, and performance is verified by the\n  benchmark workstream (order-of-magnitude parity with the inline Milvus baseline), not by\n  SLAs.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":54493,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b59d86a4638be6d4","agent_id":"a6f01f67c7f84a90b","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"59f2f526-2d21-4ded-83a4-bddff1dc3e4f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:27:31.987Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
