{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-83303d00-4562-4cdb-b723-c0357d91223a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bfa664ef38ca9a9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-x96818bq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store (branch B)\n\n> **Conditional branch epic — outcome B of the provisioning go/no-go decision.** Selected\n> only if the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for\n> RHOAI 3.5 and the BFF-managed fallback is chosen over requiring customers to bring their\n> own database. If a different outcome is selected, this epic does not exist.\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the Staff Engineer's fallback provisioning outcome: the gen-ai BFF\nprovisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for the playground directly — creating the\nPostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and\nno suitable instance exists in the namespace — following the pattern the BFF already uses\nfor guardrails infrastructure. This preserves the out-of-the-box experience without waiting\nfor the RHOAI-controlled operator layer.\n\nTwo caveats from the Staff Engineer shape the design work: **portability** (when the\nplatform-provided shared database becomes available in RHOAI 3.6+, the BFF must consume the\nshared connection string rather than self-provisioning) and **lifecycle management** (the\nBFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup on playground deletion,\nhandling of pod restarts, and credential rotation need explicit design attention).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed provisioning of the default PostgreSQL instance (existing Red Hat\n  `rhel9/postgresql-16` image, pgvector included — no custom image build): Deployment,\n  Service, PVC and credential Secret created in the playground's namespace when RAG is\n  configured and no instance exists, with the remote pgvector vector_io provider\n  configuration consuming the provisioned connection string.\n- Design and implement lifecycle handling appropriate for a non-reconciling provisioner:\n  cleanup on playground deletion, recovery from PostgreSQL pod restarts, and a credential\n  rotation approach — each documented as an explicit design decision.\n- Scope the instance per namespace (per-namespace isolation is the selected scoping model).\n- Apply resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL\n  values, adjusted by the benchmark-derived sizing recommendation.\n- Structure the provisioning behind a portability seam: the connection source (self-provided\n  vs. platform-provided shared connection string) is a swappable configuration decision, so\n  the 3.6 shared-database consumption path requires no rework of the consumer side.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG causes the BFF to provision a\n  PostgreSQL instance (pgvector included) in the playground's namespace when none exists,\n  with no manual user setup steps.\n- Playground RAG workflows (ingestion, embedding, retrieval) run end-to-end against the\n  BFF-provisioned store via the remote pgvector provider configuration.\n- Deleting the playground configuration cleans up the provisioned PostgreSQL resources\n  (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) — documented and demonstrated; pod restarts of the\n  PostgreSQL instance do not break subsequent RAG workflows; credential rotation is\n  designed and implemented.\n- The provisioned instance carries resource requests/limits derived from the\n  model-registry-operator reference values and the benchmark sizing recommendation.\n- The connection source is demonstrably swappable: supplying an external/shared connection\n  string in place of self-provisioning requires configuration only, not code changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual\n  provisioning: this epic is the delivery of that requirement under outcome B.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store:\n  end-to-end acceptance criterion above.\n- **HLR-5 (P0)** — multi-tenant scoping: per-namespace isolation of the provisioned\n  instance.\n- **HLR-6 (P0)** — lightweight operation: benchmark-derived resource sizing applied.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Fallback relationships: this branch exists only if the operator-based outcome is not\n  selected; if BFF-managed provisioning is also ruled out, the residual path is\n  bring-your-own PostgreSQL.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":57536,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b59d86a4638be6d4","agent_id":"a6f01f67c7f84a90b","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"44985771-05d6-4880-af14-161ecc6b4fa5","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:28:12.923Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
