{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-6bf2f5c0-b22a-4e98-a763-4b8b12016db3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-94c4e405c49a23ae","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-x96818bq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004.md","content":"# Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for Gen AI Studio RAG (branch C)\n\n> **Conditional branch epic — outcome C of the provisioning go/no-go decision.** Selected\n> only if neither self-provisioning path (operator-based or BFF-managed) lands for RHOAI\n> 3.5. This is the explicitly degraded fallback: it does **not** satisfy the out-of-the-box\n> requirement — customers must supply their own PostgreSQL. If a different outcome is\n> selected, this epic does not exist.\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the residual fallback outcome directed by the Staff Engineer Input:\ncustomers bring their own PostgreSQL for RAG capabilities in the playground. Users manually\nconfigure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their PostgreSQL connection details, and\nthe remote pgvector vector_io provider configuration consumes them. This aligns with the\nbreaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0, even though the playground otherwise largely\nabstracts OGX from users. The epic is small by design: the provider wiring already works\nwith any supplied pgvector endpoint; what remains is ensuring the user-provided\nconfiguration path is supported, does not fight the playground's defaults, and is\ndocumented.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Ensure the user-provided PostgreSQL configuration path works: an OGXServer instance whose\n  spec carries customer-supplied PostgreSQL connection details (via Secret reference) runs\n  playground RAG workflows against that database through the remote pgvector vector_io\n  provider configuration.\n- Ensure the playground's OGXServer-instance handling does not override or break\n  user-supplied database configuration (the configuration surface treats a supplied\n  external database as a first-class state, not an error).\n- Author user-facing documentation for bringing your own PostgreSQL: how to create the\n  connection Secret, how to set it on the OGXServer spec, and the explicit statement that\n  RAG capabilities require a user-provided database under this outcome.\n- Record the product decision context: this path degrades the out-of-the-box experience by\n  design and is aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A playground RAG workflow (ingestion, embedding, retrieval) completes successfully against\n  a customer-supplied PostgreSQL instance with pgvector, configured through the documented\n  OGXServer spec path with credentials supplied via Secret.\n- The documented procedure is reproducible end-to-end by a user following only the\n  documentation (no undocumented steps or cluster-admin improvisation).\n- The playground's instance handling provably does not clobber user-supplied database\n  configuration on reconciliation or reconfiguration.\n- Documentation states plainly that under this outcome a user-provided PostgreSQL is\n  required for playground RAG, and points production-scale users to an external vector\n  database.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store: this\n  epic keeps RAG functional under outcome C, with the database supplied by the user (the\n  \"no loss of capability\" holds only against a user-provided store — see HLR-1 note).\n- **HLR-4 (P0)** — the vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX\n  instance: the remote pgvector provider configuration with a user-managed database is the\n  strongest form of this decoupling.\n\nExplicit non-coverage (accepted trade-off, recorded in the decomposition summary's Health\nWarnings): **HLR-1** (out-of-the-box, no manual setup) is not satisfied under this outcome —\nmanual provisioning is the definition of this branch. HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping) and\nHLR-6 (sizing) also rest with the customer-supplied database rather than platform work\nunder this outcome.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Priority P1: this is the minimal residual path, deferrable relative to the P0\n  self-provisioning outcomes, and documentation-heavy rather than infrastructure work.\n- If this outcome is selected, the migration question raised in the strategy (path from the\n  default store to a production-grade vector database) is answered trivially: the\n  user already owns the database.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":58626,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b59d86a4638be6d4","agent_id":"a6f01f67c7f84a90b","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"805add62-31d9-4833-b9f3-e08039a35ac8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:28:28.793Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
