{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab9823b34b7e5a3f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go for RHOAI 3.5\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Dashboard Crimson\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 0\n12\t  local_runnability: 0\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n14\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go for RHOAI 3.5\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation resolves the central unknown of the strategy: **how the default\n30\tPostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio playground RAG is provisioned in RHOAI 3.5**.\n31\tSelf-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern requires a RHOAI-controlled\n32\toperator, which only exists once the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164,\n33\tcurrently In Progress) is delivered — a hard dependency with uncertain timing. The Staff\n34\tEngineer Input directs the development team to define a go/no-go gate for this decision as\n35\tpart of sprint planning. The deliverable is a decision record that selects exactly one of\n36\tthree bounded outcomes and gates the corresponding downstream provisioning work.\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\t- Define the go/no-go criteria and decision date for the RHOAI 3.5 provisioning approach,\n41\t  driven by the delivery status and release-fit of the dashboard operator modularization\n42\t  (RHAISTRAT-7164).\n43\t- Assess each bounded outcome against the criteria:\n44\t  - **Outcome A — operator-based self-provisioning**: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer\n45\t    lands in time; PostgreSQL lifecycle management is implemented there following the\n46\t    model-registry-operator pattern.\n47\t  - **Outcome B — BFF-managed provisioning**: the operator layer does not land in time; the\n48\t    gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC and Secret directly\n49\t    (the guardrails-infrastructure pattern), with explicit lifecycle design.\n50\t  - **Outcome C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL**: neither self-provisioning path lands; customers\n51\t    manually configure the OGXServer custom resource with their own PostgreSQL connection\n52\t    details, aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes.\n53\t- Record the release-timing assessment of the hard dependency and the risk accepted by the\n54\t  selected outcome (including the degraded out-of-the-box experience of outcome C).\n55\t- Confirm the scoping model for the selected outcome (per-namespace isolation is the\n56\t  preferred default; the per-instance alternative existed only for Auto Rag, which is out of\n57\t  scope for this strategy).\n58\t\n59\t## Acceptance Criteria\n60\t\n61\t- A written decision record exists that selects exactly one outcome (A, B or C) and states\n62\t  the go/no-go criteria, the evidence used, and the decision date.\n63\t- The record documents the delivery-status assessment of the dashboard operator\n64\t  modularization dependency that drove the selection.\n65\t- The record states the fallback that applies if the selected outcome fails later (i.e.,\n66\t  which outcome replaces it), so the switch path is pre-agreed rather than improvised.\n67\t- The selected outcome is confirmed as achievable within the RHOAI 3.5 timeframe by the\n68\t  Gen AI Studio development team (sprint-planning sign-off recorded in the decision record).\n69\t\n70\t## HLR Traceability\n71\t\n72\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — a default vector store available out-of-the-box: this investigation\n73\t  decides *which* provisioning approach delivers that requirement (or explicitly accepts the\n74\t  bring-your-own degradation), gating all provisioning work.\n75\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector\n76\t  store: the selected outcome determines the delivery path for this capability.\n77\t\n78\t## Notes\n79\t\n80\t- The go/no-go decision itself is a human sprint-planning/release decision (the Staff\n81\t  Engineer assigns it to the development team); the supporting evidence — dependency status,\n82\t  feasibility and risk of each outcome — is assembled as part of this investigation's work.\n83\t- This epic gates three conditional branch epics (one per outcome) with a\n84\t  `remove` failure impact: under a different outcome, the corresponding downstream epic does\n85\t  not exist.\n86\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9ea6f9b8cf5822b","agent_id":"a1ff60dd6c6038da7","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"bfc8903d-3a22-451b-8f40-a077c03081a7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:31:00.421Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab9823b34b7e5a3f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
