{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abbc07b71eccfade","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n3\ttitle: Capture inline Milvus baseline and benchmark pgvector for the default vector\n4\t  store\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Dashboard Crimson\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 0\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 5\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Capture inline Milvus baseline and benchmark pgvector for the default vector store\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic delivers the sprint 1 benchmark directed by the Staff Engineer Input: capture the\n35\tinline Milvus provider's performance baseline **before** inline providers are removed from\n36\tthe OGX distribution image (RHAISTRAT-7474), then verify that pgvector — the replacement\n37\tdefault vector store — performs in the same order of magnitude on typical Gen AI Studio\n38\tplayground RAG usage. It also produces the PostgreSQL resource sizing recommendation for the\n39\tdefault instance. The default pgvector instance is meant for development experimentation,\n40\tnot production usage: strict latency SLAs are explicitly not required, and the benchmark is\n41\tan order-of-magnitude comparison plus a sizing input, not a pass/fail performance gate.\n42\t\n43\tThis is Tier 1 risk-reduction work: it needs neither the provisioning-approach decision nor\n44\tthe BFF-driven configuration — a manually deployed pgvector instance exercises the remote\n45\tprovider path — but the inline Milvus baseline has a closing measurement window.\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- Define a representative benchmark workload for typical playground RAG usage (small\n50\t  document sets: ingestion, embedding, retrieval round-trips).\n51\t- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline on that workload, from the current\n52\t  distribution image, before inline provider removal lands.\n53\t- Run the same workload against a pgvector backend through the remote vector store path,\n54\t  including the flat-scan (no index) configuration that is the intended default.\n55\t- Compare results and report order-of-magnitude parity (or the gap, if any) between the\n56\t  inline Milvus baseline and pgvector.\n57\t- Produce a PostgreSQL resource sizing recommendation for the default instance, starting\n58\t  from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits as the reference\n59\t  values, adjusted by the benchmark observations.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- A benchmark report exists documenting the workload definition, the captured inline Milvus\n64\t  baseline metrics, and the pgvector measurements on the same workload.\n65\t- The report states, per metric, whether pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the\n66\t  inline Milvus baseline (the Staff Engineer's success condition — no threshold SLAs).\n67\t- The report confirms (or refutes, with evidence) that flat scan without an index is\n68\t  sufficient for typical playground document-set sizes.\n69\t- A resource sizing recommendation (requests/limits) for the default PostgreSQL instance is\n70\t  delivered, explicitly referencing the model-registry-operator values it starts from and\n71\t  the benchmark observations behind any adjustment.\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability\n74\t\n75\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — no loss of current capability: this benchmark is the measurement\n76\t  instrument for that acceptance criterion (order-of-magnitude parity with the inline\n77\t  Milvus baseline).\n78\t- **HLR-6 (P0)** — lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments: the sizing\n79\t  recommendation and flat-scan sufficiency check deliver the evidence for this requirement.\n80\t\n81\t## Notes\n82\t\n83\t- Timing risk: the inline Milvus baseline can only be measured while the inline provider is\n84\t  still present in the distribution image — coordinate with the inline-provider removal\n85\t  strategy (RHAISTRAT-7474) so the baseline is captured first. This is why the epic is P0\n86\t  despite blocking no other epic.\n87\t- Users with production-scale vector workloads are directed to an external vector database;\n88\t  the default instance's sizing targets development experimentation.\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9ea6f9b8cf5822b","agent_id":"a1ff60dd6c6038da7","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"6bc279aa-189b-4588-9450-b86fe31bf5f6","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:31:01.865Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-abbc07b71eccfade","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
