{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bcb6f8fe518b4ba0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004\n3\ttitle: Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for Gen AI Studio RAG (branch C)\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Dashboard Crimson\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tbranch: C\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: Selected only if neither self-provisioning path (operator-based\n17\t    or BFF-managed) lands for RHOAI 3.5; under any other outcome this epic is removed.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: 1\n24\t  external_dependency: 0\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 6\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for Gen AI Studio RAG (branch C)\n35\t\n36\t> **Conditional branch epic — outcome C of the provisioning go/no-go decision.** Selected\n37\t> only if neither self-provisioning path (operator-based or BFF-managed) lands for RHOAI\n38\t> 3.5. This is the explicitly degraded fallback: it does **not** satisfy the out-of-the-box\n39\t> requirement — customers must supply their own PostgreSQL. If a different outcome is\n40\t> selected, this epic does not exist.\n41\t\n42\t## Description\n43\t\n44\tThis epic delivers the residual fallback outcome directed by the Staff Engineer Input:\n45\tcustomers bring their own PostgreSQL for RAG capabilities in the playground. Users manually\n46\tconfigure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their PostgreSQL connection details, and\n47\tthe remote pgvector vector_io provider configuration consumes them. This aligns with the\n48\tbreaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0, even though the playground otherwise largely\n49\tabstracts OGX from users. The epic is small by design: the provider wiring already works\n50\twith any supplied pgvector endpoint; what remains is ensuring the user-provided\n51\tconfiguration path is supported, does not fight the playground's defaults, and is\n52\tdocumented.\n53\t\n54\t## Scope\n55\t\n56\t- Ensure the user-provided PostgreSQL configuration path works: an OGXServer instance whose\n57\t  spec carries customer-supplied PostgreSQL connection details (via Secret reference) runs\n58\t  playground RAG workflows against that database through the remote pgvector vector_io\n59\t  provider configuration.\n60\t- Ensure the playground's OGXServer-instance handling does not override or break\n61\t  user-supplied database configuration (the configuration surface treats a supplied\n62\t  external database as a first-class state, not an error).\n63\t- Author user-facing documentation for bringing your own PostgreSQL: how to create the\n64\t  connection Secret, how to set it on the OGXServer spec, and the explicit statement that\n65\t  RAG capabilities require a user-provided database under this outcome.\n66\t- Record the product decision context: this path degrades the out-of-the-box experience by\n67\t  design and is aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes.\n68\t\n69\t## Acceptance Criteria\n70\t\n71\t- A playground RAG workflow (ingestion, embedding, retrieval) completes successfully against\n72\t  a customer-supplied PostgreSQL instance with pgvector, configured through the documented\n73\t  OGXServer spec path with credentials supplied via Secret.\n74\t- The documented procedure is reproducible end-to-end by a user following only the\n75\t  documentation (no undocumented steps or cluster-admin improvisation).\n76\t- The playground's instance handling provably does not clobber user-supplied database\n77\t  configuration on reconciliation or reconfiguration.\n78\t- Documentation states plainly that under this outcome a user-provided PostgreSQL is\n79\t  required for playground RAG, and points production-scale users to an external vector\n80\t  database.\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability\n83\t\n84\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store: this\n85\t  epic keeps RAG functional under outcome C, with the database supplied by the user (the\n86\t  \"no loss of capability\" holds only against a user-provided store — see HLR-1 note).\n87\t- **HLR-4 (P0)** — the vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX\n88\t  instance: the remote pgvector provider configuration with a user-managed database is the\n89\t  strongest form of this decoupling.\n90\t\n91\tExplicit non-coverage (accepted trade-off, recorded in the decomposition summary's Health\n92\tWarnings): **HLR-1** (out-of-the-box, no manual setup) is not satisfied under this outcome —\n93\tmanual provisioning is the definition of this branch. HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping) and\n94\tHLR-6 (sizing) also rest with the customer-supplied database rather than platform work\n95\tunder this outcome.\n96\t\n97\t## Notes\n98\t\n99\t- Priority P1: this is the minimal residual path, deferrable relative to the P0\n100\t  self-provisioning outcomes, and documentation-heavy rather than infrastructure work.\n101\t- If this outcome is selected, the migration question raised in the strategy (path from the\n102\t  default store to a production-grade vector database) is answered trivially: the\n103\t  user already owns the database.\n104\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9ea6f9b8cf5822b","agent_id":"a1ff60dd6c6038da7","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"16f40d35-94e8-4849-93ab-0b4d6bff1e5c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:31:05.804Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bcb6f8fe518b4ba0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
