{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b215083a159e522c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store\n4\t  (branch B)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Dashboard Crimson\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n15\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n16\tgate_failure_impact:\n17\t  action: remove\n18\t  fallback_approach: If operator-based provisioning is selected instead, this epic\n19\t    is removed; if BFF-managed provisioning is also ruled out, provisioning falls\n20\t    back to bring-your-own PostgreSQL.\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: 1\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n25\t  existing_foundation: 1\n26\t  open_questions: 0\n27\t  external_dependency: 0\n28\t  human_process_gates: 0\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: High\n34\tai_implementability_score: 6\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\t---\n37\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store (branch B)\n38\t\n39\t> **Conditional branch epic — outcome B of the provisioning go/no-go decision.** Selected\n40\t> only if the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for\n41\t> RHOAI 3.5 and the BFF-managed fallback is chosen over requiring customers to bring their\n42\t> own database. If a different outcome is selected, this epic does not exist.\n43\t\n44\t## Description\n45\t\n46\tThis epic delivers the Staff Engineer's fallback provisioning outcome: the gen-ai BFF\n47\tprovisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for the playground directly — creating the\n48\tPostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and\n49\tno suitable instance exists in the namespace — following the pattern the BFF already uses\n50\tfor guardrails infrastructure. This preserves the out-of-the-box experience without waiting\n51\tfor the RHOAI-controlled operator layer.\n52\t\n53\tTwo caveats from the Staff Engineer shape the design work: **portability** (when the\n54\tplatform-provided shared database becomes available in RHOAI 3.6+, the BFF must consume the\n55\tshared connection string rather than self-provisioning) and **lifecycle management** (the\n56\tBFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup on playground deletion,\n57\thandling of pod restarts, and credential rotation need explicit design attention).\n58\t\n59\t## Scope\n60\t\n61\t- Implement BFF-managed provisioning of the default PostgreSQL instance (existing Red Hat\n62\t  `rhel9/postgresql-16` image, pgvector included — no custom image build): Deployment,\n63\t  Service, PVC and credential Secret created in the playground's namespace when RAG is\n64\t  configured and no instance exists, with the remote pgvector vector_io provider\n65\t  configuration consuming the provisioned connection string.\n66\t- Design and implement lifecycle handling appropriate for a non-reconciling provisioner:\n67\t  cleanup on playground deletion, recovery from PostgreSQL pod restarts, and a credential\n68\t  rotation approach — each documented as an explicit design decision.\n69\t- Scope the instance per namespace (per-namespace isolation is the selected scoping model).\n70\t- Apply resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL\n71\t  values, adjusted by the benchmark-derived sizing recommendation.\n72\t- Structure the provisioning behind a portability seam: the connection source (self-provided\n73\t  vs. platform-provided shared connection string) is a swappable configuration decision, so\n74\t  the 3.6 shared-database consumption path requires no rework of the consumer side.\n75\t\n76\t## Acceptance Criteria\n77\t\n78\t- Configuring a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG causes the BFF to provision a\n79\t  PostgreSQL instance (pgvector included) in the playground's namespace when none exists,\n80\t  with no manual user setup steps.\n81\t- Playground RAG workflows (ingestion, embedding, retrieval) run end-to-end against the\n82\t  BFF-provisioned store via the remote pgvector provider configuration.\n83\t- Deleting the playground configuration cleans up the provisioned PostgreSQL resources\n84\t  (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) — documented and demonstrated; pod restarts of the\n85\t  PostgreSQL instance do not break subsequent RAG workflows; credential rotation is\n86\t  designed and implemented.\n87\t- The provisioned instance carries resource requests/limits derived from the\n88\t  model-registry-operator reference values and the benchmark sizing recommendation.\n89\t- The connection source is demonstrably swappable: supplying an external/shared connection\n90\t  string in place of self-provisioning requires configuration only, not code changes.\n91\t\n92\t## HLR Traceability\n93\t\n94\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual\n95\t  provisioning: this epic is the delivery of that requirement under outcome B.\n96\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store:\n97\t  end-to-end acceptance criterion above.\n98\t- **HLR-5 (P0)** — multi-tenant scoping: per-namespace isolation of the provisioned\n99\t  instance.\n100\t- **HLR-6 (P0)** — lightweight operation: benchmark-derived resource sizing applied.\n101\t\n102\t## Notes\n103\t\n104\t- Fallback relationships: this branch exists only if the operator-based outcome is not\n105\t  selected; if BFF-managed provisioning is also ruled out, the residual path is\n106\t  bring-your-own PostgreSQL.\n107\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b241ea264dcfc8af","agent_id":"af08417c6040f36f6","session_id":"bb294bba-45da-456e-886f-d6e4ba682e14","uuid":"ddc11e67-f9bb-4b40-8d98-03ed40deafb9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:36:31.936Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b215083a159e522c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
